<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Walk in the Neighborhood</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is My Favorite.</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Identify the main points of talks on familiar matters.</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can listen and identify main points in talks about favorite places in the neighborhood (e.g., 'The park has swings.').
Can respond to questions about familiar places (e.g., 'What is your favorite place?' 'My favorite place is the park.').
Can listen to and recognize the main points people share about places they like (e.g., 'I like the store. It has toys.').</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or realia of different neighborhood places (park, school, store, playground, house, street, building). Students listen to the teacher say the name of a place and point to the corresponding picture. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and students respond with single words (e.g., "Park!").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the new vocabulary related to neighborhood places: <i>neighborhood, park, place, tree, flower, building, street, path, playground</i>. The teacher says each word clearly and slowly, asking students to repeat. The teacher then models simple sentences for expressing preference: "This is my favorite place. It is the park." and "I like this park because it is fun." The teacher pays special attention to the pronunciation of /b/ in 'best' and /f/ in 'favorite', asking students to listen and repeat. The teacher also models the blend of /b/ and /ɪg/ for 'big' and 'biggest' using gestures.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary and concepts.</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a <i>playground</i> a place to sleep or a place to <i>play</i>?" (Expected answer: "Play!")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something is your <i>favorite</i>, do you like it a lot or a little?" (Expected answer: "A lot!")</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Walk in the Neighborhood".</b> The teacher plays a short audio recording (or reads a short dialogue) of two children, Maria and Carlos, talking about their favorite places in their neighborhood. The dialogue introduces 8-10 target vocabulary words (<i>park, playground, street, building, tree, flower, fun, beautiful, best</i>) and grammar structures (<i>What is your favorite...? My favorite is... I like... because... This is the best...</i>).</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue (or teacher-read script) – "Maria and Carlos's Favorite Places".</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Identify the main points of talks on familiar matters.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio/dialogue twice.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listen (Gist):</b> The teacher asks, "What places do Maria and Carlos talk about?" (Students listen and may point to flashcards or say the place names).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listen (True/False):</b> The teacher provides simple True/False statements for students to mark or signal. For example: "Maria likes the <i>park</i>. True or False?" (Focus on listening for main points and specific details about preferences).</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening comprehension of favorite places and reasons. Focus on: Superlatives (<i>best</i>), Simple present + conjunction (<i>I like this park because it is fun</i>), Question structure (<i>What is your favorite?</i>), Vocabulary (<i>neighborhood, park, place, tree, flower, building, street, path, playground, fun, exciting, beautiful, favorite, best</i>). Pronunciation: /b/ in 'best', /f/ in 'favorite', phonemic awareness of 'big' - 'bigger' - 'biggest'.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Listening Practice (Listen and Circle):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with sentences that have two options. Students listen to short audio clips (or the teacher's voice) and circle the correct word or phrase that matches what they hear. Example: "I like the <i>[park / school]</i>." "This is the <i>[best / worst]</i> street." The teacher explicitly uses the vocabulary and models the pronunciation of /b/ and /f/ and the blend /b/ + /ɪg/ (e.g., "The park is big. It's the biggest park!").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Listening (Match the Description):</b> The teacher shows pictures of different neighborhood places. The teacher reads short descriptions (e.g., "It has swings and a big slide. You can run and play here. It is the best place to have fun!") and students listen, then draw a line to match the description to the correct picture (e.g., 'playground'). This reinforces listening for key details and vocabulary.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listen and Confirm):</b> Students work in pairs. Student A says, "My favorite place is the park because it has big trees." Student B listens and points to a picture of a park, then says, "Park!" or "Yes, the park." This activity allows for peer checking of listening comprehension in a supportive environment, focusing on recognizing main points and reasons using 'because'.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 15 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> "Listen to My Favorite Place!" Students listen to short audio recordings (or teacher-read descriptions) of different children describing their favorite neighborhood places and the reasons why. Each description will contain target vocabulary (e.g., <i>beautiful flowers, fun playground, big building, exciting street</i>) and grammar (<i>My favorite is... because... It is the best...</i>). For each audio clip, students are given a worksheet with 3-4 pictures of different neighborhood places. After listening, they circle the picture that matches the description and draw one small detail mentioned (e.g., a tree, a flower, a slide) or write one keyword they heard that explains *why* it's the speaker's favorite (e.g., 'fun', 'beautiful', 'big').</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Listening and identifying/drawing based on descriptions.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students actively listen to and process information about 'This Is My Favorite.' places during 'A Walk in the Neighborhood', focusing on recognizing explicit preferences and reasons.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in listening comprehension, identifying main points, and understanding reasons (using 'because') in spoken descriptions. Students will demonstrate listening for specific details rather than producing extended speech.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher observes students' ability to correctly identify places and details from audio descriptions during the Performance stage.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students listen to two new short audio descriptions of favorite neighborhood places. For each description, they will:</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Circle the picture of the place described.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Write one word or draw a small symbol that represents *why* the speaker likes that place (e.g., 'fun', 'beautiful', 'swings').</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Respond to a simple question: "What is the speaker's favorite place?" by circling the answer from two options (e.g., 'Park' / 'Street').</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible comprehension:</b> Student correctly identifies the main place (picture) at least 70% of the time.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary (in identifying answers):</b> Student identifies key descriptive words from the audio.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity:</b> Student's chosen answers (circles, drawings, keywords) are clear and legible.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences or questions:</p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can listen and understand my friend's favorite place." (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to listen to today?"</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 5 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you hear about the neighborhood today?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did listening help you understand what people like?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this listening lesson? Students seemed engaged with the audio descriptions and could identify the main points and reasons. The pronunciation practice for /b/ and /f/ and the /b/+/ɪg/ blend was effective with repetition. What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time? Perhaps provide a visual word bank for the listening tasks to aid vocabulary recall for struggling listeners. Ensure audio quality is optimal. For the next lesson, which focuses on reading comprehension, I will use a simple text describing the same neighborhood places with the same core vocabulary, allowing students to see the words they heard today. This will reinforce their understanding and prepare them for reading the content in context.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will read a short text or dialogue describing favorite neighborhood places, using the same vocabulary and grammatical structures introduced in this listening lesson. This will build on their auditory comprehension and introduce visual recognition of the words and phrases.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 5 min]</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>